+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-article">
+        <p>
+          At The Daily Grind, great coffee begins long before the first crack in our cast-iron drum roasters. Every harvest season, our sourcing team travels across the coffee belt—from the mist-covered volcanic slopes of Huehuetenango in Guatemala to the high-altitude washing stations of Yirgacheffe in Ethiopia and the lush micro-lots of Huila, Colombia. Rather than purchasing anonymous commodity lots through brokers, we operate on a strict Direct Trade model. We sit down at the cupping table with smallholder farmers and cooperative leaders, paying at least 40% above Fair Trade minimums to reward exceptional cup quality and sustainable land stewardship.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Elevation and biodiversity play a defining role in the complexity of the beans we select. We exclusively source Arabica varieties grown between 1,500 and 2,200 meters above sea level under native shade canopies. Cool mountain nights slow the maturation of the coffee cherry, allowing natural sugars and organic acids to develop densely within the seed. During harvest, pickers selectively hand-harvest only peak-ripeness cherries, passing through the same trees up to five times a season. Whether a lot undergoes classic washed fermentation in mountain spring water, honey processing on raised African drying beds, or experimental anaerobic natural fermentation, every batch is monitored for moisture content and water activity before export.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Once the parchment coffee is milled and sealed in hermetic GrainPro bags, it arrives at our local roastery where each micro-lot is sample-roasted and blind-cupped by our Q-Graders. We design custom roast profiles tailored to the cellular density and processing method of each origin—preserving the vibrant bergamot and jasmine florals of our Ethiopian heirloom lots while coaxing rich caramelized cacao and toasted hazelnut notes from our Central American bourbons. By maintaining 100% lot traceability from farm gate to roastery floor, we ensure that every bag you brew honors both the ecosystem and the hands that cultivated it.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+    </section>
